        {
          "path": "_m/g/c/p/u/royal-artillery-memorial-wp/artillery-memorial-hyde-park-c.jpg",
          "filename": "Artillery Memorial, Hyde Park Corner - geograph.org.uk - 1463123.jpg",
          "caption": "Artillery Memorial, Hyde Park Corner This piece of 1925 commemorates over 49,000 of the Royal Artilley Regiment killed in WW1. It was the work of Charles Sargeant Jagger (1885-1934) who had served in the trenches and been awarded the Military Cross. It consists of a giant Portland Stone sculpture of a howitzer with stone reliefs depicting the reality of war. The bronze figures include a dead soldier covered by a greatcoat above the inscription 'Here was a Royal fellowship of Death' from Shakespeare's Henry V. Three bronze panels by Darcy Braddell were added in 1949 in memory of the additional 30,000 killed in WWII.",
          "alt": "Artillery Memorial, Hyde Park Corner This piece of 1925 commemorates over 49,000 of the Royal Artilley Regiment killed in WW1. It was the work of Charles Sargeant Jagger (1885-1934) who had served in the trenches and been awarded the Military Cross. It consists of a giant Portland Stone sculpture of a howitzer with stone reliefs depicting the reality of war. The bronze figures include a dead soldier covered by a greatcoat above the inscription 'Here was a Royal fellowship of Death' from Shakespeare's Henry V. Three bronze panels by Darcy Braddell were added in 1949 in memory of the additional 30,000 killed in WWII.",
          "width": 640,
          "height": 427,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
          "artist": "Peter Trimming",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artillery_Memorial,_Hyde_Park_Corner_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1463123.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "royal-artillery-memorial-wp",
